[[Badlga]] is a [[Administrative divisions of Burawa#Urban Administrations|Urban Administration]] located on the Wagada Peninsula surrounded by [[Lake Haro]]. With an estimated population of 3,964,540 is the [[List of cities in Burawa|fifth-largest city]] in [[Burawa]]. Badalga was founded after the [[Great Badalga Fire]] of 1180 that had destroyed the city of [[Badalga-Jir]], which had originally held the name of Badalga. After the fire, the denizens of the original Badalga had moved to a more fortified position near the center of the Wagada peninsula on [[Orma Hill (Badalga)|Orma Hill]].

The area surrounding Badalga had always been an important stop for traders moving rare goods from the interior of Burawa to the coasts. The city became a major shipbuilding hub for Burawa in the 1600's as the deep waters of the lake and it's ease of access to the coast while being defensible had made it an important city for both military and industrial efforts for the nation. The city continued to grow to become the second largest city in Burawa by the 1908, only surpassed by [[Iskhal]] to the south.

Badalga is a major hub for finance, culture, commerce, industry, education, technology, telecommunications, and transportation. It is the home of the headquarters of major manufacturing companies such as [[Buksom]] and [[Tael Heavy Industries]]. 

== History ==
=== Early History ===
The Wagada Peninsula had been occupied by humans since [[Ancient Ufyen]] times, the hills along the center of the peninsula had always provided strategic advantage to the general lowlands around [[Lake Haro]].

The area became under the control of the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] in 924 during the [[Northern Conquest of Burawa]] and a military fort was established around the same year. In 1180 the [[Great Badalga Fire]] had caused an influx of people from the neighboring [[Badalga-Jir]] to take refuge in the fort ontop of [[Orma Hill (Badalga)|Orma Hill]], eventually moving the town itself to the area. 

=== Middle Ages ===
Records of the time before the Empire of Burawa had seen the city's population grow to around 20,000 people. During the [[First Orma-Burawa War]] in 1204, the city had gone under siege by the [[Kingdom of Orma]] and was captured for 2 years before it was restored under Burawai control. The city was converted into a [[Hoggaanka]] by decree of [[Marcel II]], the king of Burawa around 1306, to be run by the [[Hedga Clique]], first [[Iohn Hedga]].

The city became a major shipbuilding hub in the mid 1300s as ships needed for trans-oceanic transport had become highly desired. The relatively deep bay that formed around the tip of the peninsula became home to many shipbuilding firms established from investment from merchants from [[Iskhal]].